{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~5–15 EUR), especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path when recent momentum is negative.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: the model is slow to recognize regime changes; it continues forecasting sideways-to-up while the market sells off. If the last 5–10 days show a clear decline, impose additional negative drift for the next 1–3 weeks.",
    "Underestimates drawdown depth: realized declines are sharper and more persistent than forecast. Increase downside convexity—i.e., amplify negative returns when recent volatility is elevated and momentum is negative.",
    "Horizon-dependent: error grows with horizon; the back half (days ~15–30) is most over-optimistic and often even reverts upward while reality keeps falling. Enforce stronger mean reversion toward a lower anchor (e.g., recent 10–20d average minus a risk premium) rather than reverting upward.",
    "Overshoot after local rebounds: when history contains a small bounce within a broader selloff, the model extrapolates recovery too strongly. Cap rebound magnitude and speed; require multiple consecutive up days before allowing an upward slope in the forecast.",
    "Volatility mismatch: forecast paths are smoother than outcomes; real series has step-down moves. Add a ‘selloff shock’ adjustment (one-off downward step) when recent 10d range is large and prices are making lower lows."
  ]
}
